nvk: Upload shaders with the copy engine and invalidate instruction caches.

Shader heap ranges are recycled: nvk_shader_destroy() returns a shader's range
to the heap (nvk_shader.c:1086-1090) and the next nvk_heap_upload() can place
another shader, of any stage, at the same address. With a full BAR the heap is
CPU-mapped write-combined and uploads are a plain memcpy (nvk_device.c:333-339,
nvk_heap.c:125-126, nvk_mem_arena.c:204), and nothing ever invalidates the
GPU's instruction and program-header caches: command buffers invalidate only
constants (nvk_cmd_draw.c:798-800). A draw can then run bytes cached for the
range's previous user. The fault this matches on GB202 is Xid 13 "Shader
Program Header 11/18 Error" (ESR 0x405840=0xa0040800) under ANGLE dEQP-GLES:
bit 18 is a ShaderType that is not PS in the fragment slot and bit 11 a clear
imap_system_values_ab bit 31, i.e. a well-formed vertex header read at a
fragment program address. The fault is rare and has no deterministic
reproducer, so this change makes NVK publish programs the way the proprietary
driver does rather than claiming a demonstrated fix.

The proprietary driver (libnvidia-eglcore 615.78.08, GB202):
 - keeps the shader heap in video memory that is never CPU-mapped and copies
   every program from system-memory staging with the copy engine, LAUNCH_DMA
   0x186 (NON_PIPELINED, FLUSH_ENABLE, pitch to pitch), followed by a
   semaphore release;
 - counts committed uploads per device (0xd494a0, lock addl);
 - in the first submission on a channel whose last published count differs
   (0xd6a235), acquires the copy's release (host semaphore, ACQ_GEQ with
   acquire switch, 0xd6a790) and then emits INVALIDATE_SHADER_CACHES_NO_WFI
   with INSTRUCTION set (0xd6a503; the compute class form at 0xd6a520 on a
   channel without 3D), with no wait-for-idle or memory barrier. It also resets
   its MME shadow of the bound program offsets so every program address is
   sent again; NVK has no such shadow: every command buffer re-emits all of its
   shaders' addresses before its first draw (shaders_dirty = ~0,
   nvk_cmd_draw.c:860) and dispatches carry the address in the QMD.
nvc0 likewise invalidates after every upload (MEM_BARRIER 0x1011,
nvc0_program.c:944-945).

Do the same: the shader heap is never CPU-mapped (the existing upload-queue
path, nvk_heap.c:127-152, now on every system), nvk_heap counts uploads on
every path, each queue remembers the count it last invalidated for, and
nvk_queue_submit_exec() samples the count, flushes the upload queue, waits for
it and only then pushes the queue state with the invalidate, ahead of the
command buffers.

Not in Mesa main as of fc88667ecb8c (2026-10-15).

diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_device.c b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_device.c
index 537535dbd03c..80cf584b03ac 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_device.c
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_device.c
@@ -350,17 +350,22 @@ nvk_CreateDevice(VkPhysicalDevice physicalDevice,
             goto fail_samplers;
       }
 
-      /* If we have a full BAR, go ahead and do shader uploads on the CPU.
-       * Otherwise, we fall back to doing shader uploads via the upload queue.
+      /* Shader uploads always go through the upload queue (copy engine), and
+       * the heap has no CPU mapping, also with a full BAR.  This is the
+       * proprietary driver's model (libnvidia-eglcore 615.78.08): its shader
+       * heap is video memory that is never CPU-mapped, each program is copied
+       * from system-memory staging by the copy engine (LAUNCH_DMA
+       * NON_PIPELINED | FLUSH_ENABLE, the form nvk_upload_queue_upload()
+       * emits) and the 3D channel acquires the copy's semaphore release
+       * before its first use (0xd6a790).  Program bytes then reach video
+       * memory only through the copy engine, in device-wide upload order,
+       * never through a write-combined BAR1 mapping.
        *
        * Also, the I-cache pre-fetches and NVIDIA has informed us
        * overallocating shaders BOs by 2K is sufficient.
        */
-      enum nvkmd_mem_map_flags shader_map_flags = 0;
-      if (pdev->info.bar_size_B >= pdev->info.vram_size_B)
-         shader_map_flags = NVKMD_MEM_MAP_WR;
       result = nvk_heap_init(dev, &dev->shader_heap,
-                             NVKMD_MEM_LOCAL, shader_map_flags,
+                             NVKMD_MEM_LOCAL, 0 /* map_flags */,
                              2048 /* overalloc */,
                              pdev->info.cls_eng3d < VOLTA_A);
       if (result != VK_SUCCESS)
diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_heap.c b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_heap.c
index da31183d79cf..b16450db2646 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_heap.c
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_heap.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include "nvk_queue.h"
 
 #include "util/macros.h"
+#include "util/u_atomic.h"
 
 #include "nv_push.h"
 #include "nv_push_cl90b5.h"
@@ -27,6 +28,7 @@ nvk_heap_init(struct nvk_device *dev, struct nvk_heap *heap,
    assert(overalloc < NVK_MEM_ARENA_MIN_SIZE);
    heap->overalloc = overalloc;
    util_vma_heap_init(&heap->heap, 0, 0);
+   heap->upload_seq = 0;
 
    return VK_SUCCESS;
 }
@@ -151,6 +153,15 @@ nvk_heap_upload(struct nvk_device *dev, struct nvk_heap *heap,
       }
    }
 
+   /* Counted on both paths, after the bytes are written or their copy is
+    * recorded and before the address is returned: any submission that can
+    * reference this upload samples a count that includes it.  A range handed
+    * out here may have held another program whose header and code the GPU
+    * still caches; queues invalidate the instruction caches when the count
+    * moved (nvk_queue_state_update).
+    */
+   p_atomic_inc(&heap->upload_seq);
+
    return VK_SUCCESS;
 }
 
